// GitHub Copilot CLI user-level settings.
//
// Comments are JSONC line comments on purpose. A `"//"` string key is the other
// obvious way to annotate a JSON config, and Copilot REJECTS it with
// `Warning: Ignoring unknown top-level key(s) in user settings file ... "//"`.
// These `//` comments parse silently. Verified both ways against the live CLI.
//
// HOOKS MUST LIVE HERE, NOT IN config.json. Put them in config.json and they
// appear to work exactly once, after which the CLI migrates them out, logs
// `Settings migration: "hooks" differs...` and deletes them -- leaving the
// devcontainer guard silently gone with nothing to notice. config.json says as
// much in its own header ("User settings belong in settings.json. This file is
// managed automatically."), so it is deliberately never symlinked.
// install.py symlinks THIS file to ~/.copilot/settings.json, and copilot/hooks
// to ~/.copilot/hooks.
//
// THAT SYMLINK IS NOT DURABLE, AND THESE COMMENTS DO NOT SURVIVE THE CLI. Copilot
// writes settings atomically -- temp file plus rename -- so when it saves settings
// it REPLACES ~/.copilot/settings.json with a regular file rather than writing
// through the link. Observed twice against an isolated COPILOT_HOME. Two
// consequences, both mundane to trigger (`copilot skill add`, `copilot plugin
// install`, `/memory on|off`, `/settings ...` all do it):
//   * Every `//` comment in this file is destroyed and the hook schema is
//     normalized. The hook block itself survived and kept firing in both tests, so
//     the guard does not silently die -- but the annotation explaining it does.
//   * From that moment this file is NO LONGER the source of truth for the live
//     config. Edits here to the matcher, the timeout, the hook path or the key
//     below are inert until install.py runs again.
// Nothing leaks the other way: the CLI does not write into this repo. install.py
// checks for the detached case on every run and warns loudly; to check by hand,
// `ls -l ~/.copilot/settings.json` and confirm it is still a symlink into
// ~/code/dotfiles/copilot/. To repair it, re-run install.py.
{
  // NO CO-AUTHORED-BY TRAILERS. This is not cosmetic, and it is not a setting
  // restating prose -- it is the one mechanical enforcement this file can give a
  // rule that is otherwise entirely unenforced under Copilot.
  //
  // claude/CLAUDE.md forbids AI-attribution lines in any artifact, and under
  // Claude Code claude/hooks/block-ai-attribution.sh enforces that. That hook is
  // NOT ported to Copilot. Worse, the Copilot CLI defaults the other way and
  // actively instructs the agent to add the trailer: its own settings registry
  // describes this key as "Add a Co-authored-by trailer to commits", and app.js
  // wires it as `coauthorEnabled: T.includeCoAuthoredBy !== !1` -- anything other
  // than an explicit `false` enables it. Omitting the key is therefore not
  // neutral, it opts in.
  //
  // Verified against the shipped v1.0.80 runtime: `includeCoAuthoredBy` is on the
  // CLI's canonical top-level settings key list (runtime.node's
  // userSettingsKeysJson), so it is accepted here and does not trip the
  // "Ignoring unknown top-level key(s)" warning.
  "includeCoAuthoredBy": false,

  "hooks": {
    "preToolUse": [
      {
        // MATCHER IS `.*` DELIBERATELY -- do not narrow it back to a name list.
        //
        // It used to be an exact-name allowlist
        // (bash|create|edit|apply_patch|str_replace_editor), assembled from the
        // tool names observed across a few model families plus one unverified
        // guess. That is a silent fail-open: any tool name Copilot uses that is
        // not on the list gets NO hook at all, and the list's completeness cannot
        // be verified against a packed binary whose tool roster is not
        // enumerable. Matching everything collapses the risk from "this name list
        // must stay complete forever" to "the shim's case statement must classify
        // correctly" -- testable code under our control, where a miss is visible.
        //
        // The cost is one short process per tool call: the shim exits 0 after a
        // single jq spawn (~16ms measured) for every out-of-scope name -- view,
        // grep, glob, skill, sql, web_fetch and the rest.
        //
        // Matchers compile as `^(?:PATTERN)$` (Rust regex, full match). An
        // INVALID one makes the CLI log `Invalid matcher regex ... hook will be
        // skipped` and run nothing at all, which is another fail-open. Any edit
        // here must be re-verified by observing an actual block, not by reading.
        //
        // AN UNPARSEABLE *FILE* IS THE SAME FAILURE CLASS AND STRICTLY WORSE,
        // BECAUSE IT IS COMPLETELY SILENT. Observed: with one syntax error
        // injected into ~/.copilot/settings.json, the CLI printed NO warning and
        // NO error, the hook never ran, and a write landed in a guarded repo. The
        // invalid matcher above at least logs a line to look for; a parse failure
        // logs nothing at all, so the only symptom is a session that is quietly
        // unguarded. (An unknown top-level KEY does warn -- "Ignoring unknown
        // top-level key(s)" -- and trailing commas are tolerated. It is
        // specifically the parse failure that is silent.) This file is
        // hand-maintained JSONC with ~80 comment lines, so that is a live risk on
        // every edit rather than a hypothetical one. install.py therefore parses
        // this file on EVERY run and fails loudly if it does not parse, catching a
        // broken edit at install time instead of via a bypass nobody sees. Do not
        // remove that check, and re-run install.py after editing this file rather
        // than assuming the guard survived.
        //
        // OUT OF SCOPE, NOT A GAP TO CLOSE: MCP tool calls never fire preToolUse.
        // They raise a separate `preMcpToolCall` event that no preToolUse matcher
        // sees, however wide -- `.*` does not reach them and nothing in this entry
        // can. Do not try to fix that from here.
        "matcher": ".*",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "bash \"$HOME/.copilot/hooks/require-devcontainer.sh\"",

            // TIMEOUT IS PINNED, NOT INHERITED, AND THE FAIL-OPEN BEHIND IT IS
            // REAL. A preToolUse hook that times out has its tool call ALLOWED
            // through, and no setting makes a timeout fail closed. That is
            // confirmed, not inferred: the shipped v1.0.80 runtime
            // (node_modules/@github/copilot-linux-x64/prebuilds/linux-x64/runtime.node)
            // carries the message template `timed out; allowing the tool call to
            // proceed:` in its string table, right beside `Denied by preToolUse
            // hook:` and the errored-hook wording `(fail-closed):`. Errors fail
            // closed; timeouts do not. Since the bound cannot be removed it is at
            // least stated explicitly here rather than inherited from a default a
            // CLI release can change under us. Measured runtimes (median, this
            // machine): ~14ms for an out-of-scope tool, ~83ms for a bash call,
            // ~41ms per additional path in a multi-file apply_patch, ~8.3s for a
            // 200-path one. Those are the ORDINARY costs; the worst cases are
            // below and are an order of magnitude larger.
            //
            // The numbers that do the real work are NOT this one -- they are the
            // shim's own MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES, MAX_TARGET_PATHS and
            // GUARD_BUDGET_SECONDS, each of which DENIES on exceed. Be accurate
            // about what they achieve: they make the fail-open HARD TO REACH, they
            // do NOT make it unreachable.
            //
            // RE-MEASURED TWICE, AND BOTH EARLIER FIGURES HERE WERE WRONG IN THE
            // FLATTERING DIRECTION (~10s, then ~19.9s). Worst case MEASURED is
            // ~20.4s against this 30s; the CEILING the shim's arithmetic actually
            // permits is JUST UNDER 21s, in both directions. That is a margin of
            // roughly 1.43x, not 3x and not 1.5x -- and 21s is the number to cite,
            // because the measurements are only a lower bound on it.
            // Both worst cases are set by GUARD_BUDGET_SECONDS (20s) rather than by
            // the payload ceiling, and the extra second past 20 is not slop: the
            // budget is checked at the TOP of each guard iteration so an
            // already-started spawn can overshoot it, and the per-spawn deadline is
            // derived from bash's whole-second `SECONDS`, so a spawn beginning at
            // 19.99s elapsed is handed a full extra second and dies at ~21.0s. The
            // shim's own header carries the full measured table and the arithmetic.
            // Treat 20 and 30 as a matched pair. A slower machine, or a wedge
            // `timeout` cannot interrupt, could still cross 30s and be allowed
            // unguarded. The ordering is the invariant: every bound in the shim
            // stays BELOW this timeoutSec. Change one, change all, keep the gap.
            "timeoutSec": 30
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
